WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.930
With medical and recreational marijuana legalization
spreading across the country

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.620
Reason attended the Cannabis World Congress
and Business Exposition in New York City

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:13.240
to get a sense of where things are headed
with commercialized weed

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.040
and weed related products.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.360
We encountered retired pro football players
promoting non psychoactive CBD oils

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.980
that they say have replaced the opioids they
once needed to dull their pain.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.320
If it wasn't for a company like Elixinol and
the products that we produce

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.920
I don't know where I'd be right now.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.620
We sampled cold brewed coffee infused with
a marijuana extract that won't get you stoned

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.960
but still makes for a calmer morning.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.219
It helps minimize the jitters in cold brew
coffee.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.820
We learned why candy is the go-to product
for edibles.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.480
If you're smoking and you get the munchies
and you want that extra high

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.580
what are you gonna go for?

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:52.040
A quick snack, so you have your edibles and
you have your weed, you're elevated.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:56.120
And we talked to an attorney who's going state
by state where weed is legal

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:00.800
to fight for laws that clear away the criminal
records of nonviolent drug offenders.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.180
If you're going to make something legal

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:07.260
continuing to burden them with a lifelong
criminal record makes no sense.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.440
But by far the most vision inducing part of
the conference was keynote speakers Billy Tauzen.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.120
He's a former conservative congressman from
Louisiana who went on to head up PhRMA

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.220
the pharmaceutical industry's massive lobbying
group.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.900
Now he works for Lenitiv Labs, a medical cannabis
company

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:31.300
founded by TV host, MS sufferer and long time
legalization advocate Montel Williams.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.520
Tauzen himself survived a life threatening
bout with cancer

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.740
and now he says he wishes he could have smoked
weed to ease his pain.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.720
Took 65 pounds off of me, beat the hell outta
me,

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.660
I'm watching patients today take advantage
of medical marijuana products

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:48.790
and get through that a lot easier.

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:52.520
And I'm thinking 'wow, why didn't we have
that opportunity when I went through it

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:53.860
15 years ago.'

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:56.890
Do all former congressmen have to get cancer

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:00.440
before we're gonna get medical marijuana or
recreational marijuana?

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:04.800
God I hope not and I hope most members of
Congress don't have to go through that.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.300
I told the group today I think we took a silent
vote, a secret ballot,

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:11.780
we'd win them more easily.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.680
If you were a member of Congress and you've
been taught all your life that this is a bad entry drug

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.100
that's gonna get your children addicted and
they're gonna become a life of drug addicts

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.380
or what have you

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.520
you've got some convincing on your hands,
right?

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.400
You've gotta somehow say 'no that's not what
we're talking about

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.900
we're talking about adult use, we're talking
about medical use,

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:36.060
we're talking about something very different
that's been kept from you and the public for years

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:40.400
by simply bad policy and sort of dark age
thinking.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.580
We somehow have to educate them that it's
literally in their interest to make changes in policy

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.400
Why? because their constituents want them
to.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.620
You were also the head of PhRMA, the lobbying
group for the pharmaceutical industry

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:58.069
a lot of people in the legalization space
are worried that big pharma

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:01.000
is going to try to keep marijuana from becoming
legal.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.720
Are those concerns warranted?

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.360
The pharmaceutical industry is all about finding
new medicines

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.980
and when it's legally possible for them to
go to the FDA with medical marijuana products

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.360
they're going to be doing that.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.340
As I told the group today they're probably
gonna want to come around and see you.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.260
If you've already pioneered these medicines
and you've got them in the marketplace.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.740
They're going to want to talk to you about
acquiring your companies one day.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:25.600
They're in that business for Christ's sake.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.360
No I don't think they're gonna fight it, I
think they're gonna probably watch, wait and see

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.760
if the legalization spreads as I think it
will around the country

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.060
congress wakes up and, you know, passes the
laws that take it off the schedules

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:45.560
and let them become investigators and researchers
into the exact why and how it works,

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:48.620
I think they're going to be partners with
this group today.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:52.200
You just got to literally take some time for
it to happen.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.320
Tauzen has never been a full on libertarian.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.360
But his experiences have taught him a valuable
lesson about self ownership.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.819
And he supports the right to try bill recently
signed by Donald Trump

00:04:02.819 --> 00:04:07.050
giving terminally ill patients access to experimental
medications.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:11.920
Libertarians have it right there, we ought
to have a right to our own body and our own protection

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.120
if I want to try something new I'll have the
right to try it.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.310
I hate the notion that, as I told the group
today, that the medicine that saved my life

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:25.460
is off label and the company can't tell other
people that it could be useful pancreatic medicine

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.640
they can only use it for the label, that it's
labeled for.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.000
At least we ought to open that up

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.220
we ought to let people talk about the real life experiences with medicines

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:37.440
and which ones help which people.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.620
One thing I've learned is how different we
are biologically

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.140
and the medicine that's going to work for
you may not work for me

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.800
we ought to be able to tell those stories,
be able to share them on the internet,

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.120
learn from them.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:51.340
Yeah there's going to be some mistakes maybe
but I'd love to live in that world

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.660
rather than one that denies us access to something
that could save our lives.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:00.300
Donald Trump has said, at the urging of Cory
Gardner, Republican senator from Colorado

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:05.680
that he would sign a bill returning marijuana
laws and regulation to the states

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.620
are you confident that he will do that if
presented with that

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:14.320
and, you know, will Congress actually write
something and vote on something?

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.440
Two good questions, as to the first:

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.120
I think one day Congress will, if we literally
advocate properly

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:24.620
I think they wanna do that, it's just a question
of giving them the courage to do it.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:26.260
As to Trump keeping his word?

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:29.480
You may not like him one bit, but he keeps
his word.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:32.780
And whatever he told you in that campaign
he's following through on it.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.960
To a lot of people's consternation.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.310
He's doing exactly what he said he would.

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:41.160
When he says he'd sign that bill, I have no
doubt he'd sign it.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:46.320
One final question, what's the timeline for
full legalization of marijuana?

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:50.360
Is it ten years, is it fifteen years, is it
already happening and you know,

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:54.310
we'll go outside of this convention center
and it'll be a done deal?

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:57.360
I've learned something about the progress
of mankind

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.320
any time you try to predict it's gonna take
longer than you think

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:02.440
you're probably wrong.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:06.930
This is moving fast, this tide has turned,
we're past the tipping point

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:10.400
ain't no coming back, I think this is going
to happen within five years.

